 \section{Associative entities}\label{sec:assoc}
 
+The primary key of each associative entity is composed of the primary keys of the entities it connects,
+so each one participates in two identifying relationships. Genre Probability connects Track Score to
+Genre, so its key is the two-part key of Track Score followed by Genre ID. No associative entity has a key of its own.
+Each key component is marked Unique = No, because a single component repeats freely; only the
+combination is unique. Two of the associative entities also carry a third relationship, which gives
+the model its two ternary structures: Track, Scoring Model and Genre meet at Track Score, and Listener,
+Track and Station meet at Play. In both cases the third foreign key stays outside the primary key.
+Genre ID is excluded because the top genre is determined by the pair of recording and model, so adding
+it to the key would permit two top genres for one pair. Station ID is excluded because it is optional,
+and a primary key column can never be empty. Play is the one entity whose foreign keys alone do not
+identify a row, because a listener can stream the same recording many times. Played At completes its
+key, in the same way that a partial key completes the key of a weak entity.
+
